# {{PROJECT_NAME}}

A Groundhog counter contract with a small React + Vite frontend. Contract calls
use generated TypeScript bindings and a temporary native wallet in the browser.

## Prerequisites

- Node.js 22.12+ and npm.
- Groundhog CLI installed and configured with `groundhog init`.
- Rust with rustup and access to the Groundhog Cargo registry.
- Access to the Groundhog npm packages. Configure registry authentication using
  the [TypeScript client instructions](https://github.com/groundhogchain/client-ts#registry-auth-one-time-setup).
- A compatible local runtime; install it with `groundhog toolchain install`.

## Run

Start the node in another terminal:

```sh
groundhog node
```

From this project:

```sh
npm install
npm run dev
```

Startup builds the contract and bindings, funds the configured CLI wallet, deploys
and initializes a fresh counter, then starts Vite. Open the printed URL.
The browser generates a private key in memory, derives a native account, and
requests an airdrop before enabling the buttons. Keys are never written to disk.

Each page reload creates a new wallet. The contract and its state remain for the
dev-server session. Restarting `npm run dev` creates a new counter starting at zero.
If you restart the node, restart `npm run dev` too. Ctrl-C stops the app; your node
keeps running. Decrementing zero demonstrates a rejected transaction.

Startup uses the `local` profile. `GROUNDHOG_RPC_URL` overrides its endpoint;
`PORT` overrides Vite's default 5173. `GROUNDHOG_CLI` selects a different CLI binary.

## Project layout

- `src/` and `tests/`: Rust contract and native/RV32/integration tests, shared with the counter template.
- `web/main.tsx`: counter UI and transaction feedback.
- `web/wallet.ts`: temporary browser wallet and funding.
- `web/generated/counter.ts`: generated bindings, refreshed on build/startup.
- `scripts/`: thin CLI build/deploy orchestration and Vite startup.
- `.groundhog/app.json`: public configuration for the current local deployment.

## Check your changes

```sh
cargo test
groundhog test
npm run build
```

`npm run build` rebuilds the bindings, checks TypeScript, and bundles the frontend.
The example runs locally through `npm run dev`, which supplies `/deployment.json`
and proxies `/rpc` to the node. Hosting the bundle elsewhere requires providing
those endpoints and an appropriate wallet flow.
